ot underneath the rule of  reason we could think of many other  examples having to do with other  emotions having to be you know present  with another person when you're feeling  sad or restraining the joy that you feel  over something that you think you  shouldn't feel joyous about and we could  go on and on and on  Hume is saying that these sorts of  conflicts always turn out to be one by  the side of passion as a matter of fact  you might say it's a no contest so he  says that you know we really improperly  speak of the combat of passion and  reason here's the passage reason is and  ought to be only the slave of the  passions and then he says and can never  pretend to any other office than to  serve and obey them now isn't that a  rather strange thing to say if it's so  intuitively obvious that this is the  case that reason can never do anything  other than serve and obey the passions  if that's the case then perhaps reason  is only a rationalization of what it is  that our emotions or drives or desires  or passions are bidding us to do and  whom says I realize that this is  somewhat extraordinary as an opinion so  we should confirm it by some other  considerations now he's saying other  considerations because he's already  brought up one really important  consideration and that is that reason  has as he calls it no original  influence on the will and thereby on our  actions that means that reason is not  able to originate not able to be the the  origin or font or you know a beginning  place for any of the decisions that we  make doesn't mean that reason plays no  role it just means that there always has  to be some emotion some desire some  thing that's moving us and then reason  discovers proper means to that end or  looks around for how you know something  can be brought about or compares things  against each other  perhaps that's we have a desire for  something and reason says aha this will  satisfy that desire but reason by itself  is unable to motivate us to do anything  according to two humans he brings up  another consideration which has to do  with the nature of passions what is a  passion he tells us a passion is an  original existence isn't that an  interesting turn of phrase he says or if  you will modification of existence now  what does he mean by that a passion is  something that is real it's something  that we feel within ourselves it's part  of our experience and it is what Hume in  other places will call an impression  there's a difference between the idea of  anger or a memory of being angry and  then actually feeling angry or feeling  desire or feeling fear and we could go  on and on through the entire list of  emotions if we want to by calling it an  original it means that it's not a copy  of anything else it is what it is and it  originates itself you might say we feel  what we feel  without it being merely you know  something that that is being produced  remembering that feeling in the past so  he says it doesn't have any  representative quality which renders it  a copy of any other existence or  modification this is a very important  point for Humes argument because it  lacks any representative quality the  anger the the hatred the sadness the  envy the the you know joy the desire the  love that I feel just is the emotion  that I feel he also says that here we go  when I am angry I am actually possessed  with the passion in that emotion have no  more a reference to any other object  than when I am thirsty or sick or more  than five feet high so not only does it  not have a representative quality human  so far as to say that these have no  reference to an object you might say  well that's a little strange  because when I'm happy I'm happy because  of X or X makes me happy humans say well  that's the cause of it but the emotion  itself you're not thinking about that  thing that you're feeling the feeling  right that's what the emotion is even  anger which is definitely an emotion  oriented towards something you're  feeling the anger and then you're just  kind of looking for things to be angry  at and you know the the anger itself  doesn't have any necessary reference to  an object for Hume that may be a little  bit of a stretch for some people when it  comes to philosophy of emotion but it's  very important to think about what this  actually gets him because there's no  representative quality and no reference  to an object  this means that strictly speaking reason  and passion they're not even on the same  playing field and they can't be opposing  each other so to use the anger example  he says tis impossible that this passion  can be a  opposed by or contradictory to truth and  reason since this contradiction consists  in the disagreement of ideas considered  as copies with those objects which they  represent so insofar as were feeling the  emotion the emotion itself cannot be  unreasonable  it cannot be contrary to reason it also  can't be reasonable it's just not  working in the same dimension as reason  or truth for that matter Hume would be  perfectly willing to say that our  feelings are our feelings and we feel  them and they're not even like true or  false insofar as they're our feelings  they just are their original existences  as he said now that presents a view of  reason and emotion and Moton and  motivation and we'll and human desire  and actions that seems to put reason  entirely aside and maybe in that case  reason really only can be the slave of  the passions first we have to feel  something we have to desire something or  be averse to something and only after  that can reason show up on the scene  sort of like a servant saying what is it  that you want you know boss or my lord  or you know I pick whatever lo Q shinu  you desire for that right but there's a  little bit more to the story the way  that humans is telling it to us here he  does tell us that emotions can in fact  be accompanied by a judgment or an  opinion and he says that nothing can 