n  I was working third shift around two  o'clock after you know having had a lot  to drink and he had found going back to  his car that the parking structure was  completely locked up and he could not  get his car and he was telling us about  it and we looked at him and we and we  said buddy  that's not our pocket parking structure  here's the number you can call but  you're going to get an answering machine  because it  new years and I don't got very upset  angry sad you know but but much of what  he was doing was quite unreasonable  none of his emotions were going to make  anything better he wasn't going to  affect us because we couldn't do  anything whatsoever and you're not going  to get in touch with anybody new year's  day to try to get your car out of hock  so we suggested to me find a hotel and  stay there  and I don't know what became of the man  but that would be a perfect example of  basing one's emotional reactions on  things that don't really exist they're  not really the case what's the other way  in which our passions can go wrong in  terms of the judgments that are being  made Hume says that sometimes we can  select insufficient means for bringing  about what it is that we desire or are  trying to avoid so it says in exerting  any passion in action we choose means  insufficient for the designed end and  deceive ourselves in our judgment of  causes and effects so if I'm in love  with somebody and really want to go out  with this person and I think that's  showing up at their workplace with the  boom box above my head like in that  silly 1980s movie is somehow going to  move that person to reciprocate my love  I am choosing means that are clearly  insufficient to that end I might do  better to I don't know ask the person  out or bring flowers or do something  like that another good example of  something you know perhaps at the other  end if I think that I'd like to go out  with somebody because I'm feeling  amorous towards them and I send them a  carefully worded email in the form of a  memo perhaps that's an insufficient  means as well and we could go on and on  and on you know people do this in terms  of anger thinking that the actions that  they're selecting are the proper means  for expressing or act  that anger only to find out that they've  gone way too far or attack the wrong  person or didn't go far enough  and didn't actually affect the person so  Hume thinks that those sorts of  judgments made following an emotion they  can certainly be off-base they can they  can be in conflict reason they can be  false now if that's really the case he  tells us that reason can in fact  demonstrate this to us  and these passion then will yield to  reason so it's not really the case for  Hume that that it's always still black  and white reason is and ought to be only  the slave of the passions sometimes  reason is sort of a slave in the sense  that it's looking out for the passions  best interests and helping those unruly  passions to find their way to something  better  and sometimes those passions will  actually yield to reason he gives you a  prime example he tells us that a passion  can never in any sense be called  unreasonable but when found in a false  opposition or when it chooses means  insufficient for the designed end it's  impossible that reason and passion can  ever oppose each other or dispute for  the government of the will in actions  how so reason tells the passions knock  it off he says the moment we perceive  the falsehood of any stop position or  the insufficiency of any means our  passions yield to our reason without any  position so in some respects Hume is you  know completely consistent in saying  that reason is always the slave oven  serves the passions but it serves it in  such a way that it might actually in  some cases bring about the cessation of  a passion that would seem to be opposing  reason but it'll be because we desire  something else instead so you could say  that passion still rules